#| msgid ""
#| "Maneuvering Prediction Program (MPP-1.3)\n"
#| "M. G. Parsons, December, 1994\n"
#| "1. METHODS:\n"
#| "1.1  This program offers two main options: Linear Evaluation which implements the\n"
#| "methods proposed by Clarke, Gedling, and Hine (1) for the  assessment  of  course\n"
#| "stability,  turning  ability,  and  controllability  by  a  helmsman; and Turning\n"
#| "Prediction which  implements  the  multiple linear regression equations presented\n"
#| "by Lyster and Knights (2) for the estimation of turning circle characteristics.\n"
#| "1.2  The  user should consult the Clarke paper prior to using the program. If the\n"
#| "Linear  Evaluation option is used, the user can also obtain the linearized maneu-\n"
#| "vering equations of motion in state variable form (3).\n"
#| "1.3  In the Linear Evaluation option, the linear derivatives are estimated using\n"
#| "the multiple linear regression equations presented by Clarke et al (1). The velo-\n"
#| "city derivatives  were obtained from 72 sets of data from rotating arm and planar\n"
#| "motion  tests.  Acceleration  derivatives  were  obtained from planar motion test\n"
#| "data. The  independent  variables  include  T/LWL, CB*B/T, B/LWL, and B/T. Clarke\n"
#| "feels  that  these  results  are  at  least  as  effective  as the semi-empirical\n"
#| "equations developed by Wagner Smitt, Norrbin, or  Inoue. Water  depth corrections\n"
#| "were added  by  regressing  the  data  presented by Fugino (4) for the Tokyo Maru\n"
#| "( CB = 0.805 ) and the  Mariner ( CB = 0.589 ). Independent  variables  in  these\n"
#| "analyses were CB and inverse powers of F = (H/T) -1. These  can be used for water\n"
#| "depths down to H/T= 1.3, but are considered unreliable below this value. The trim\n"
#| "corrections presented by Inoue (5) are used  for  the  velocity  derivatives. The\n"
#| "independent variable  in  these corrections  is the trim to mean draft ratio. The\n"
#| "various  time  constants  and  gains  in  the  first-  and  second-order Nomoto's\n"
#| "equations are calculated from the linear derivatives.\n"
#| "1.4  The quantities calculated to allow evaluation of the maneuvering performance\n"
#| "of the vessels are as follows:\n"
#| "1.4.1 Inverse time  constant (1/T')  and  inverse  gain  (1/K'). Turning  ability\n"
#| "increases  with  decreasing 1/K' while course stability and quickness of response\n"
#| "to helm increases with 1/T'.\n"
#| "1.4.2 Clarke's turning index P.This quantity is derived from the linear equations\n"
#| "as  the  amount  of  turn achieved in one ship length using a unit step rudder. A\n"
#| "minimum value of 0.3 (equivalent to about 10 degrees for a 35 degree step rudder,\n"
#| "within the  limits  of  the  linearized  equations of motion) is recommended with\n"
#| "values as low as 0.2 considered acceptable for large tankers.\n"
#| "1.4.3 Bare hull  dynamic  stability  criterion C. This must be positive for open-\n"
#| "loop, controls-fixed course stability of the bare hull.\n"
#| "1.4.4 Phase margin applicable to the closed-loop steering system.The phase margin\n"
#| "of the ship transfer function, including the steering gear, is calculated. Nomoto\n"
#| "notes  that  by  anticipating  the  ships  turning, a typical helmsman can apply\n"
#| "between 20 and 30 degrees of phase advance.  Thus, the  ship  with  steering gear\n"
#| "cannot have more than this amount of negative phase margin  for  the  combination\n"
#| "of the ship and helmsman to be closed-loop stable on course. Thus, a phase margin\n"
#| "greater than about -20 degrees is recommended for reasonable human control.\n"
#| "1.5 All  output  is  nondimensional  following Clarke (1) except the phase margin\n"
#| "which is expressed in degrees.\n"
#| "1.6 In the completely independent Turning Prediction option, the regression equa-\n"
#| "tions developed by Lyster and Knights (2) from sea trial results are used to  es-\n"
#| "timate turning  characteristics for a user-input rudder angle and approach speed.\n"
#| "The  dependence  of the turns of single screw ships on the direction of propeller\n"
#| "rotation and the ratio of the mean draft to the design draft were not implemented.\n"
#| "1.7 The option produces estimates of the following:\n"
#| "- steady turning diameter (meters)\n"
#| "- tactical diameter (meters)\n"
#| "- advance (meters)\n"
#| "- transfer (meters)\n"
#| "- steady speed in the turn (knots)\n"
#| "1.8  This model is for  deep water and includes no dependence on water depth. The\n"
#| "range of the ship parameters included in regression data set is as follows:\n"
#| "+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
#| "| Parameter   |   Single screw    |       Twin screw        |\n"
#| "+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
#| "|     Lpp     |  54,9 ... 329,2 m |    76,2 ... 225,6 m     |\n"
#| "|     Cb      |  0,56 ... 0,87    |    0,42 ... 0,62        |\n"
#| "|rudder angle |  10,0 ... 45 degr.|    10,0 ... 35 degr.    |\n"
#| "|     L/B     |  5,56 ... 9,09    |    5,00 ... 16,67       |\n"
#| "|   trim/Lpp  |  0,00 ... 0,05    |   -0,01 ... 0,03        |\n"
#| "|     AR      |  0,01 ... 0,04    | 0,01 ... 0,02 per rudder|\n"
#| "|     AB      | -0,11 ... 0,04    |   -0,10 ... 0,0         |\n"
#| "|     Fr      |  0,06 ... 0,298   |   0,074 ... 0,655       |\n"
#| "+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
#| "This model should be used with CAUTION outside this range.\n"
#| "2.   INPUT:\n"
#| "2.1  The input to MPP is through a series as follows:\n"
#| "- LWL length on waterline in meters\n"
#| "- B maximum beam on waterline in meters\n"
#| "- TF draft forward in meters\n"
#| "- TA draft aft in meters\n"
#| "- CB block coefficient on LWL\n"
#| "- LCG longitudinal center of gravity in %LWL from amidships; + forward\n"
#| "- GYRAD yaw radius of gyration k33 as a fraction of LWL; default value 0.25\n"
#| "- AB difference between the submerged bow profile area  on  the  centerplane  and\n"
#| "that of a plumb bow as fraction of LWL*T; i.e.,AB = area forward of STA0/(LWL*T),\n"
#| "positive when a bulbous bow projects forward of  the  forward  perpendicular,  or\n"
#| "AB = cutaway  area  aft  of  STA0/(LWL*T), negative  with a raked stem or cutaway\n"
#| "forefoot.\n"
#| "2.2  Steering Characteristics obtains the rudder area, steering gear  time  cons-\n"
#| "tant, position of the rudder center of effort (typically the 1/4 chord point from\n"
#| "the leading edge), and selection of single or twin screw. The rudder area can  be\n"
#| "input either as a fraction of LWL*T or automatically as the minumum  rudder  area\n"
#| "specified  by  Det  Norske  Veritas for commercial vessels. For single screw, the\n"
#| "sterm  type  must  be  designated  as a closed or open stern. For twin screw, the\n"
#| "number of rudders must be designated as one or two.\n"
#| "2.2.1  AR total rudder  area  as fraction of LWL*T; i.e., AR = A/(LWL*T);  option\n"
#| "is calculated from the Det Norske Veritas minimum rudder area\n"
#| "2.2.2  TE steering gear time constant in seconds; default value is 2.5 s\n"
#| "2.2.3  XR center of effort of rudder in %LWL from amidships;+ aft;i.e.,about 49.0\n"
#| "2.3    Operating Conditions obtains the water depth and the initial vessel speed.\n"
#| "2.3.1  H/T water depth to ship draft ratio (H/T); input 1000 for deep water\n"
#| "2.3.2  VK initial or reference ship speed in knots\n"
#| "3. REFERENCES:\n"
#| "1. Clarke  D., Gedling  P., and Hine  G., \"The Application of Manoevring Criteria\n"
#| "in Hull Design Using Linear Theory\", Transactions RINA, Vol. 124, 1982.\n"
#| "2. Lyster  C. A., and Knights  H. L., \"Prediction  Equations  for  Ships' Turning\n"
#| "Circles\", Transactions NECIES 1978-1979.\n"
#| "3. Parsons M. G., \"Informal  Notes for NA530 Automatic Control in Naval Architec-\n"
#| "ture  and  Marine  Engineering\", Vol. I, \"Classical Control,\" and Vol. II \"Modern\n"
#| "Control.\"\n"
#| "4. Fugino  M., \"Maneuverability in Restricted Waters: State of the Art\", The Uni-\n"
#| "versity of Michigan NAME Report No. 184, Aug., 1976.\n"
#| "5. Inoue, S., Hirano, M., and Kijima, K., \"Hydrodynamic Derivatives on Ship Mano-\n"
#| "evring\", International Shipbuilding Progress, Vol. 28, No. 321, 1981."
msgid ""
"Maneuvering Prediction Program (MPP-1.3)\n"
"M. G. Parsons, December, 1994\n"
"1. METHODS:\n"
"1.1  This program offers two main options: Linear Evaluation which implements the\n"
"methods proposed by Clarke, Gedling, and Hine (1) for the  assessment  of  course\n"
"stability,  turning  ability,  and  controllability  by  a  helmsman; and Turning\n"
"Prediction which  implements  the  multiple linear regression equations presented\n"
"by Lyster and Knights (2) for the estimation of turning circle characteristics.\n"
"1.2  The  user should consult the Clarke paper prior to using the program. If the\n"
"Linear  Evaluation option is used, the user can also obtain the linearized maneu-\n"
"vering equations of motion in state variable form (3).\n"
"1.3  In the Linear Evaluation option, the linear derivatives are estimated using\n"
"the multiple linear regression equations presented by Clarke et al (1). The velo-\n"
"city derivatives  were obtained from 72 sets of data from rotating arm and planar\n"
"motion  tests.  Acceleration  derivatives  were  obtained from planar motion test\n"
"data. The  independent  variables  include  T/LWL, CB*B/T, B/LWL, and B/T. Clarke\n"
"feels  that  these  results  are  at  least  as  effective  as the semi-empirical\n"
"equations developed by Wagner Smitt, Norrbin, or  Inoue. Water  depth corrections\n"
"were added  by  regressing  the  data  presented by Fugino (4) for the Tokyo Maru\n"
"( CB = 0.805 ) and the  Mariner ( CB = 0.589 ). Independent  variables  in  these\n"
"analyses were CB and inverse powers of F = (H/T) -1. These  can be used for water\n"
"depths down to H/T= 1.3, but are considered unreliable below this value. The trim\n"
"corrections presented by Inoue (5) are used  for  the  velocity  derivatives. The\n"
"independent variable  in  these corrections  is the trim to mean draft ratio. The\n"
"various  time  constants  and  gains  in  the  first-  and  second-order Nomoto's\n"
"equations are calculated from the linear derivatives.\n"
"1.4  The quantities calculated to allow evaluation of the maneuvering performance\n"
"of the vessels are as follows:\n"
"1.4.1 Inverse time  constant (1/T')  and  inverse  gain  (1/K'). Turning  ability\n"
"increases  with  decreasing 1/K' while course stability and quickness of response\n"
"to helm increases with 1/T'.\n"
"1.4.2 Clarke's turning index P.This quantity is derived from the linear equations\n"
"as  the  amount  of  turn achieved in one ship length using a unit step rudder. A\n"
"minimum value of 0.3 (equivalent to about 10 degrees for a 35 degree step rudder,\n"
"within the  limits  of  the  linearized  equations of motion) is recommended with\n"
"values as low as 0.2 considered acceptable for large tankers.\n"
"1.4.3 Bare hull  dynamic  stability  criterion C. This must be positive for open-\n"
"loop, controls-fixed course stability of the bare hull.\n"
"1.4.4 Phase margin applicable to the closed-loop steering system.The phase margin\n"
"of the ship transfer function, including the steering gear, is calculated. Nomoto\n"
"notes  that  by  anticipating  the  shipÂs  turning, a typical helmsman can apply\n"
"between 20 and 30 degrees of phase advance.  Thus, the  ship  with  steering gear\n"
"cannot have more than this amount of negative phase margin  for  the  combination\n"
"of the ship and helmsman to be closed-loop stable on course. Thus, a phase margin\n"
"greater than about -20 degrees is recommended for reasonable human control.\n"
"1.5 All  output  is  nondimensional  following Clarke (1) except the phase margin\n"
"which is expressed in degrees.\n"
"1.6 In the completely independent Turning Prediction option, the regression equa-\n"
"tions developed by Lyster and Knights (2) from sea trial results are used to  es-\n"
"timate turning  characteristics for a user-input rudder angle and approach speed.\n"
"The  dependence  of the turns of single screw ships on the direction of propeller\n"
"rotation and the ratio of the mean draft to the design draft were not implemented.\n"
"1.7 The option produces estimates of the following:\n"
"- steady turning diameter (meters)\n"
"- tactical diameter (meters)\n"
"- advance (meters)\n"
"- transfer (meters)\n"
"- steady speed in the turn (knots)\n"
"1.8  This model is for  deep water and includes no dependence on water depth. The\n"
"range of the ship parameters included in regression data set is as follows:\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"| Parameter   |   Single screw    |       Twin screw        |\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"|     Lpp     |  54,9 ... 329,2 m |    76,2 ... 225,6 m     |\n"
"|     Cb      |  0,56 ... 0,87    |    0,42 ... 0,62        |\n"
"|rudder angle |  10,0 ... 45 degr.|    10,0 ... 35 degr.    |\n"
"|     L/B     |  5,56 ... 9,09    |    5,00 ... 16,67       |\n"
"|   trim/Lpp  |  0,00 ... 0,05    |   -0,01 ... 0,03        |\n"
"|     AR      |  0,01 ... 0,04    | 0,01 ... 0,02 per rudder|\n"
"|     AB      | -0,11 ... 0,04    |   -0,10 ... 0,0         |\n"
"|     Fr      |  0,06 ... 0,298   |   0,074 ... 0,655       |\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"This model should be used with CAUTION outside this range.\n"
"2.   INPUT:\n"
"2.1  The input to MPP is through a series as follows:\n"
"- LWL length on waterline in meters\n"
"- B maximum beam on waterline in meters\n"
"- TF draft forward in meters\n"
"- TA draft aft in meters\n"
"- CB block coefficient on LWL\n"
"- LCG longitudinal center of gravity in %LWL from amidships; + forward\n"
"- GYRAD yaw radius of gyration k33 as a fraction of LWL; default value 0.25\n"
"- AB difference between the submerged bow profile area  on  the  centerplane  and\n"
"that of a plumb bow as fraction of LWL*T; i.e.,AB = area forward of STA0/(LWL*T),\n"
"positive when a bulbous bow projects forward of  the  forward  perpendicular,  or\n"
"AB = cutaway  area  aft  of  STA0/(LWL*T), negative  with a raked stem or cutaway\n"
"forefoot.\n"
"2.2  Steering Characteristics obtains the rudder area, steering gear  time  cons-\n"
"tant, position of the rudder center of effort (typically the 1/4 chord point from\n"
"the leading edge), and selection of single or twin screw. The rudder area can  be\n"
"input either as a fraction of LWL*T or automatically as the minumum  rudder  area\n"
"specified  by  Det  Norske  Veritas for commercial vessels. For single screw, the\n"
"sterm  type  must  be  designated  as a closed or open stern. For twin screw, the\n"
"number of rudders must be designated as one or two.\n"
"2.2.1  AR total rudder  area  as fraction of LWL*T; i.e., AR = A/(LWL*T);  option\n"
"is calculated from the Det Norske Veritas minimum rudder area\n"
"2.2.2  TE steering gear time constant in seconds; default value is 2.5 s\n"
"2.2.3  XR center of effort of rudder in %LWL from amidships;+ aft;i.e.,about 49.0\n"
"2.3    Operating Conditions obtains the water depth and the initial vessel speed.\n"
"2.3.1  H/T water depth to ship draft ratio (H/T); input 1000 for deep water\n"
"2.3.2  VK initial or reference ship speed in knots\n"
"3. REFERENCES:\n"
"1. Clarke  D., Gedling  P., and Hine  G., \"The Application of Manoevring Criteria\n"
"in Hull Design Using Linear Theory\", Transactions RINA, Vol. 124, 1982.\n"
"2. Lyster  C. A., and Knights  H. L., \"Prediction  Equations  for  Ships' Turning\n"
"Circles\", Transactions NECIES 1978-1979.\n"
"3. Parsons M. G., \"Informal  Notes for NA530 Automatic Control in Naval Architec-\n"
"ture  and  Marine  Engineering\", Vol. I, \"Classical Control,\" and Vol. II \"Modern\n"
"Control.\"\n"
"4. Fugino  M., \"Maneuverability in Restricted Waters: State of the Art\", The Uni-\n"
"versity of Michigan NAME Report No. 184, Aug., 1976.\n"
"5. Inoue, S., Hirano, M., and Kijima, K., \"Hydrodynamic Derivatives on Ship Mano-\n"
"evring\", International Shipbuilding Progress, Vol. 28, No. 321, 1981"
msgstr ""
"Programa de predicción de maniobrabilidad (MPP-1.3)\n"
"M. G. Parsons, diciembre de 1994\n"
"1. MÉTODOS:\n"
"1.1  Este programa ofrece dos opciones principales: la evaluación lineal, que implementa\n"
"los métodos propuestos por Clarke, Gedling y Hine (1) para la evaluación de la\n"
"estabilidad de rumbo, la capacidad de giro y la controlabilidad por un timonel; y la predicción\n"
"de giro, que implementa las ecuaciones de regresión lineal múltiple presentadas\n"
"por Lyster y Knights (2) para la estimación de las características del círculo de giro.\n"
"1.2  El usuario debe consultar el artículo de Clarke antes de utilizar el programa. Si\n"
"se utiliza la opción de evaluación lineal, el usuario también puede obtener las ecuaciones linealizadas del\n"
"movimiento de maniobra en forma de variables de estado (3).\n"
"1.3  En la opción de evaluación lineal, las derivadas lineales se estiman mediante las\n"
"ecuaciones de regresión lineal múltiple presentadas por Clarke et al (1). Las derivadas de velo-\n"
"cidad se obtuvieron a partir de 72 conjuntos de datos de ensayos de brazo giratorio y de movimiento plano. Las derivadas de\n"
"aceleración se obtuvieron a partir de los datos de ensayos de movimiento plano.\n"
"Las variables independientes incluyen T/LWL, CB*B/T, B/LWL y B/T. Clarke\n"
"considera que estos resultados son al menos tan eficaces como las ecuaciones semiempíricas\n"
"desarrolladas por Wagner Smitt, Norrbin o Inoue. Las correcciones por profundidad de agua\n"
"se añadieron mediante regresión de los datos presentados por Fugino (4) para el Tokyo Maru\n"
"( CB = 0.805 ) y el Mariner ( CB = 0.589 ). Las variables independientes en estos\n"
"análisis eran CB y potencias inversas de F = (H/T) -1. Estas pueden usarse para profundidades de agua\n"
"profundidades hasta H/T= 1.3, pero se consideran poco fiables por debajo de este valor. Las correcciones de trimado\n"
"presentadas por Inoue (5) se usan para las derivadas de velocidad. La\n"
"variable independiente en estas correcciones es la relación entre el trimado y el calado medio. Las\n"
"diversas constantes de tiempo y ganancias en las ecuaciones de Nomoto de primer y segundo orden se calculan a partir de las derivadas lineales.\n"
"ecuaciones se calculan a partir de las derivadas lineales.\n"
"1.4  Las magnitudes calculadas para permitir la evaluación del comportamiento en maniobra\n"
"de las naves son las siguientes:\n"
"1.4.1 Constante de tiempo inversa (1/T') y ganancia inversa (1/K'). La capacidad de giro\n"
"aumenta al disminuir 1/K', mientras que la estabilidad de rumbo y la rapidez de respuesta\n"
"al timón aumentan con 1/T'.\n"
"1.4.2 Índice de giro de Clarke P. Esta magnitud se deriva de las ecuaciones lineales\n"
"como la cantidad de giro alcanzada en una eslora de buque con un timón de escalón unitario. Se\n"
"recomienda un valor mínimo de 0,3 (equivalente a unos 10 grados para un timón de escalón de 35 grados,\n"
"dentro de los límites de las ecuaciones de movimiento linealizadas) y se\n"
"consideran aceptables valores tan bajos como 0,2 para grandes petroleros.\n"
"1.4.3 Criterio C de estabilidad dinámica del casco desnudo. Debe ser positivo para la estabilidad de rumbo del casco desnudo en lazo abierto con los controles fijos.\n"
"en lazo abierto con los controles fijos.\n"
"1.4.4 Margen de fase aplicable al sistema de gobierno en lazo cerrado. El margen de fase\n"
"de la función de transferencia del buque, incluido el servomotor del timón, se calcula. Nomoto\n"
"señala que, anticipando el giro del buque, un timonel típico puede aplicar\n"
"entre 20 y 30 grados de avance de fase. Por lo tanto, el buque con servomotor del timón\n"
"no puede tener más que esta cantidad de margen de fase negativo para que la combinación\n"
"de buque y timonel sea estable en rumbo en lazo cerrado. Por lo tanto, se recomienda un margen de fase\n"
"superior a unos -20 grados para un control humano razonable.\n"
"1.5 Todas las salidas son adimensionales según Clarke (1), excepto el margen de fase\n"
"que se expresa en grados.\n"
"1.6 En la opción completamente independiente de predicción del giro, las ecuacio-\n"
"nes de regresión desarrolladas por Lyster y Knights (2) a partir de resultados de pruebas de mar se usan para esti-\n"
"mar las características de giro para un ángulo de timón y una velocidad de aproximación introducidos por el usuario.\n"
"La dependencia de los giros de los buques de hélice única respecto al sentido de rotación de la hélice\n"
"y la relación entre el calado medio y el calado de proyecto no se implementaron.\n"
"1.7 La opción produce estimaciones de lo siguiente:\n"
"- diámetro de giro estacionario (metros)\n"
"- diámetro táctico (m)\n"
"- avance (m)\n"
"- transferencia (m)\n"
"- velocidad estable en el giro (nudos)\n"
"1.8  Este modelo es para aguas profundas y no incluye dependencia de la profundidad del agua.\n"
"el rango de los parámetros del buque incluidos en el conjunto de datos de regresión es el siguiente:\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"| Parámetro   |   Hélice única    |       Dos hélices       |\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"|     Lpp     |  54,9 ... 329,2 m |    76,2 ... 225,6 m     |\n"
"|     Cb      |  0,56 ... 0,87    |    0,42 ... 0,62        |\n"
"| ángulo de timón |  10,0 ... 45 degr.|    10,0 ... 35 degr.    |\n"
"|     L/B     |  5,56 ... 9,09    |    5,00 ... 16,67       |\n"
"|   trim/Lpp  |  0,00 ... 0,05    |   -0,01 ... 0,03        |\n"
"|     AR      |  0,01 ... 0,04    | 0,01 ... 0,02 per rudder|\n"
"|     AB      | -0,11 ... 0,04    |   -0,10 ... 0,0         |\n"
"|     Fr      |  0,06 ... 0,298   |   0,074 ... 0,655       |\n"
"+-------------+-------------------+-------------------------+\n"
"Fuera de este rango, este modelo debe utilizarse con PRECAUCIÓN.\n"
"2.   ENTRADA:\n"
"2.1  La entrada a MPP se realiza mediante la siguiente serie:\n"
"- LWL eslora en la flotación en metros\n"
"- B manga máxima en la flotación en metros\n"
"- TF calado a proa en metros\n"
"- TA calado a popa en metros\n"
"- CB coeficiente de bloque en LWL\n"
"- LCG centro longitudinal de gravedad en %LWL desde la sección media; + hacia proa\n"
"- GYRAD radio de giro en guiñada k33 como fracción de LWL; valor por defecto 0,25\n"
"- AB diferencia entre el área del perfil de proa sumergido en el plano de crujía y\n"
"- la de una roda vertical como fracción de LWL*T; es decir, AB = área a proa de STA0/(LWL*T),\n"
"- positiva cuando un bulbo de proa sobresale por delante de la perpendicular de proa, o\n"
"AB = área recortada a popa de STA0/(LWL*T), negativa con roda inclinada o pie de roda recortado\n"
"pie de roda.\n"
"2.2  Las características de gobierno obtienen el área del timón, la constante de tiempo del servomotor,\n"
"la posición del centro de presión del timón (normalmente el punto a 1/4 de la cuerda desde el borde de ataque),\n"
"así como la selección de hélice simple o doble. El área del timón puede\n"
"se introduce como fracción de LWL*T o automáticamente como área mínima del timón\n"
"especificada por Det Norske Veritas para buques mercantes. Para una hélice,\n"
"el tipo de popa debe designarse como popa cerrada o abierta. Para dos hélices,\n"
"el número de timones debe designarse como uno o dos.\n"
"2.2.1  AR área total del timón como fracción de LWL*T; es decir, AR = A/(LWL*T); la opción\n"
"se calcula a partir del área mínima del timón de Det Norske Veritas\n"
"2.2.2  TE constante de tiempo del servomotor del timón en segundos; valor por defecto 2,5 s\n"
"2.2.3  XR centro de esfuerzo del timón en %LWL desde la medianía; + hacia popa; es decir, unos 49,0\n"
"2.3    Condiciones de operación obtiene la profundidad del agua y la velocidad inicial del buque.\n"
"2.3.1  H/T relación profundidad del agua / calado del buque (H/T); introducir 1000 para aguas profundas\n"
"2.3.2  VK velocidad inicial o de referencia del buque en nudos\n"
"3. REFERENCIAS:\n"
"1. Clarke  D., Gedling  P., and Hine  G., \"The Application of Manoevring Criteria\n"
"in Hull Design Using Linear Theory\", Transactions RINA, Vol. 124, 1982.\n"
"2. Lyster  C. A., and Knights  H. L., \"Prediction  Equations  for  Ships' Turning\n"
"Circles\", Transactions NECIES 1978-1979.\n"
"3. Parsons M. G., \"Informal  Notes for NA530 Automatic Control in Naval Architec-\n"
"ture  and  Marine  Engineering\", Vol. I, \"Classical Control,\" and Vol. II \"Modern\n"
"Control.\"\n"
"4. Fugino  M., \"Maneuverability in Restricted Waters: State of the Art\", The Uni-\n"
"versity of Michigan NAME Report No. 184, Aug., 1976.\n"
"5. Inoue, S., Hirano, M., and Kijima, K., \"Hydrodynamic Derivatives on Ship Mano-\n"
"evring\", International Shipbuilding Progress, Vol. 28, No. 321, 1981"

